# Cloudflare Web Analytics site token.
#
# LEAVE THIS UNSET IN PRODUCTION. Web Analytics is configured for automatic
# injection, so Cloudflare adds the beacon to the HTML at the edge and the
# page already reports without any help from the build. Setting this would
# add a second beacon and double-count every visit.
#
# It exists as a fallback: edge injection depends on Cloudflare being able to
# rewrite the response, so if it ever stops working, setting this variable
# restores measurement without a code change.
#
# Set it under Settings > Build > Build variables and secrets. It is read at
# build time, so the runtime Settings > Variables & Secrets page is the wrong
# one -- and that page refuses this Worker anyway, having no script to inject
# anything into.
#
# To check whether edge injection is working, request the live site
# with a browser User-Agent and grep for '"token":' -- Cloudflare does not
# rewrite HTML for a plain curl, so the naive check reports a false negative.
#
# The value is the site TOKEN, from Analytics & Logs > Web Analytics >
# Manage site > JS snippet, where it appears as data-cf-beacon='{"token":
# "..."}'.
#
# It is NOT the siteTag in the dashboard URL. Cloudflare gives every Web
# Analytics site two different identifiers -- site_tag identifies it in the
# dashboard, site_token goes in the beacon -- and they look alike enough
# (both 32 hex characters) to be mistaken for two separate sites.
PUBLIC_CF_BEACON_TOKEN=
